is removed on the common path.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"State explicitly that the spawn-elimination is contingent on `.store-compat.json` (or an event dir) being present on disk, and record the residual: a `.git`+HEAD store with no on-disk structure (e.g. a legacy zero-ticket store) still spawns git per read — either accept and document it, or note why it is out of scope.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.336,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'only falls back to the bounded git rev-parse ... probe when no store structure is present.'","compat.py _init_ensures docstring: 'an ABSENT record is implicit-legacy and passes through' — so a legacy store may carry no committed record.","store_usability.py _carries_store_structure returns False when neither COMPAT_FILENAME exists nor any well-formed event dir is present, so store_is_usable falls to the git probe for such a store."],"finding":"The plan's per-read spawn elimination is contingent on `.store-compat.json` (or an event dir) being present on disk, and yields NO improvement for a store that has `.git`+resolvable-HEAD but carries no on-disk structure — e.g. a legacy pre-21dd store with an absent (implicit-legacy) compat record and zero tickets (hence no event dir); that store still spawns git on every read. The plan neither states this contingency nor scopes the residual case.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f2889147ec82d8a85","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"Decision / Implementation Plan","norm_id":"nc26fb98eabfabeb0","priority":0.264,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A legacy (pre-21dd) tracker with .git + resolvable HEAD, no committed .store-compat.json, and zero tickets: reads still spawn git each time.","Any initialized store whose working tree has not yet had .store-compat.json checked out."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In the Decision / Implementation Plan, add a sentence noting that a live initialized clone always carries the committed `.store-compat.json` in its working tree (stamped by `_init_ensures._store_compat_unit`, survives materialize), so the common read path short-circuits; and flag the residual `.git`+HEAD-but-structureless (legacy zero-ticket) case as still-spawning-but-accepted.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Collapse the conditional ACs to the chosen branch: keep the reduce-by-structure ACs (decision recorded + measurement drop + regression tests unchanged) and mark the caching-detection and accept-as-is ACs not-applicable given the Decision section's commitment.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5b","T5e"],"criteria":["COH","E1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.3737,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["AC: 'If reduced by caching: a test proves a store that becomes unusable DURING a session is detected'","AC: 'If accepted as-is: the reasoning is recorded on store_usability.py'","Decision: 'Choose the reduce-by-durable-structure mechanism, not caching.'"],"finding":"The acceptance criteria are written as a conditional decision fork (accept-as-is vs reduce; if-caching), but the Decision/Implementation Plan already commits to reduce-by-durable-structure and explicitly rejects caching — leaving AC line 3 (caching-detection test) and AC line 4 (record accept-as-is reasoning) as dead branches that no described work reaches.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f31f8f61f74925eba","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance criteria vs Decision / Implementation Plan","norm_id":"n1b8bc25aa20763ee","priority":0.2263,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"State that the decision is 'reduce by durable structure', drop the caching and accept-as-is conditional ACs, and keep only the measurement-drop + unchanged-regression-test ACs.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8571,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Ground the 'avoid caching a filesystem health predicate' claim with a concrete basis (e.g. this repo's own staleness-hazard reasoning already stated in the ticket, or a named source) instead of an unattributed 'best-practice precedent'.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.5114,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'Best-practice precedent is to avoid caching a filesystem health predicate whose whole job is to notice state changes, and to prefer a cheap durable sentinel already committed in the store before spawning a subprocess.'","Web corroboration (retrieved): general caching-staleness trade-off guidance (thecodeforge.io 'Caching Strategies — TTL Pitfalls and Staleness Trade-Offs') and health-check guidance that checks should verify real state (upstat.io / adrianbailador health-check guides) support the PRINCIPLE, so the claim is not fabricated or contradicted — only unattributed.","The mechanism itself is grounded in-repo (store_usability.py:138-159 _carries_store_structure / .store-compat.json; the existing bounded git runner), so no novel architecture is being introduced."],"finding":"The Decision/Implementation Plan asserts 'Best-practice precedent is to avoid caching a filesystem health predicate whose whole job is to notice state changes, and to prefer a cheap durable sentinel' as established precedent, but cites no source; the claim is a prior-art/best-practice assertion left unattributed.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9fb936c59f10c56d","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Decision / Implementation Plan (first paragraph)","norm_id":"n908cbc33034ab28f","priority":0.1886,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A future reader re-litigating whether caching would have been acceptable finds only an unsourced 'best-practice precedent' assertion."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Replace the bare 'Best-practice precedent is to...' phrasing with the repo-local rationale already present in the ticket — a cached 'usable' verdict is precisely the staleness hazard the parent bug (rapt-dreadable-dromedary) exists to remove — which is self-grounding and needs no external citation.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7143,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Confirm (and record) whether a store WITH committed rebar structure but a broken/corrupt git repo should be usable under the reorder, and verify no existing broken/mid-clone fixture in test_reads_uninitialized_store.py carries committed structure that would short-circuit past the git probe.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5b","T5e"],"criteria":["T4","E6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.7,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'reorder store_is_usable so it first accepts a tracker that carries rebar store structure, and only falls back to the bounded git rev-parse ... probe when no store structure is present'","Plan: 'existing broken, gitless snapshot, and mid-clone tests remain unchanged'","A broken-git store WITH committed structure now hits the structure path before the probe — a semantics change the plan does not enumerate among its case walkthrough (marker-only / mid-clone / gitless)."],"finding":"Structure-first reordering short-circuits BEFORE the git HEAD probe, which changes behavior for a store that carries committed rebar structure (e.g. valid .store-compat.json) but has a broken/corrupt git repo: today the always-run git probe would raise store_uninitialized, but under the reorder it would be judged usable. The plan asserts existing broken/mid-clone tests 'remain unchanged' without confirming none of those fixtures carry committed structure.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8384573788e1e5a1","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Decision / Implementation Plan + File impact (store_usability.py)","norm_id":"n583b6fcac580f8d8","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Enumerate the 'structure-present + broken-git' case explicitly in the case walkthrough and add/point to a test pinning its expected verdict.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"no","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"insufficient"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.75,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"State explicitly that the reordered store_is_usable preserves the `_has_git` (.git-existence) gate BEFORE the HEAD probe in the fallback path, so a `.git`-less tracker nested in a code checkout still fails rather than resolving the enclosing repo's HEAD.","cohort":["E4"],"criteria":["E4"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.662,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'reorder store_is_usable so it first accepts a tracker that carries rebar store structure, and only falls back to the bounded git rev-parse --verify HEAD probe when no store structure is present'","Plan assertion: 'a marker-only broken store still has neither structure nor resolvable HEAD and raises store_uninitialized'","store_usability.py:167-171: 'The .git-existence check MUST precede the HEAD probe: git -C tracker rev-parse walks UP to an enclosing repository, so probing HEAD on a .git-less tracker nested inside a code checkout would resolve the WRONG (parent) HEAD and report a broken store as usable.'","test_reads_uninitialized_store.py:213-228 (_tracker_present_without_git): 'The tracker is deliberately nested inside a git-initialised code repo so the guard's ordering matters... The predicate must reject on the absent .git BEFORE it ever probes HEAD.'"],"finding":"The reorder mechanism rests on an unstated load-bearing premise: that the `.git`-existence gate (`_has_git`) still precedes the HEAD probe in the reordered fallback path. The plan asserts 'a marker-only broken store still has neither structure nor resolvable HEAD and raises', but that only holds if `_has_git` still guards the probe. The current code makes this ordering MANDATORY (store_usability.py:167-171 docstring: `git -C tracker rev-parse` walks UP to an enclosing repo, so probing HEAD on a `.git`-less tracker resolves the WRONG parent HEAD and reports a broken store as usable; test `_tracker_present_without_git` nests the broken tracker inside a git repo precisely to pin this). The plan's Implementation Plan and File impact never mention `_has_git` or the walk-up constraint.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f059e2d445f8a71e6","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Decision / Implementation Plan; File impact (src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py)","norm_id":"n9fe2207a9eda1eff","priority":0.088,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A production marker-files-only broken tracker (`.tickets-tracker/` with no `.git`) nested inside the code checkout: if the reorder drops the `_has_git` precondition, `_head_resolves` walks up to the enclosing code repo's HEAD, returns True, and the broken store reads as usable instead of raising store_uninitialized."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Amend the Implementation Plan to specify the reordered predicate as `isdir AND (_carries_store_structure OR (_has_git AND _head_resolves))`, and note in File impact that the `.git`-existence precondition on the HEAD probe (store_usability.py:174) is retained verbatim to preserve the walk-up guard pinned by `_tracker_present_without_git`.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3333,"ve